!!! Request targets: percent-decoding, query strings and form bodies.
!!!
!!! One module, because a query string and an urlencoded form body are the
!!! same grammar arriving by two routes, and a framework that decoded them
!!! differently would be a framework with two sets of bugs.
!!!
!!! Everything here works in bytes. nil.std.text measures and slices text by
!!! byte — `length` of "café" is 5 — which is what a percent-escape needs,
!!! since %C3%A9 is two escapes for one character and must decode to two bytes
!!! that happen to land next to each other.

book nilweb.http.uri

read nil.std.core
read nil.std.text
read everything from nilweb.text.scan


public query-parameter begin record
    field name as text
    field content as text
end record


!!! --- Decode one percent-encoded component.
!!!
!!! `%C3%A9` is two escapes that together are one character, so the escapes
!!! cannot be turned into text one at a time. The converter this has to go
!!! through — nil.std.core's i32_to_char — takes a Unicode scalar, not a byte:
!!! asked for 195 it gives back the two bytes that encode U+00C3, and decoding
!!! escape by escape would turn "café" into "cafÃ©". So the escapes are
!!! gathered as byte values and assembled into characters by bytes-as-text
!!! below, which is where the UTF-8 is actually read.
!!!
!!! Literal stretches are copied as they stand. A byte that is part of a
!!! multi-byte character is copied one byte at a time, which is correct
!!! because joining text joins bytes: the character is reassembled by the
!!! concatenation.
!!!
!!! `+` becomes a space, because this decodes both query strings and form
!!! bodies and a form body spells space that way. A `%` that is not followed
!!! by two hex digits is kept literally rather than dropped: the request is
!!! malformed, and inventing a deletion loses the evidence of what arrived.

public decode-component receives raw as text returns text begin paragraph
    value width is nil.std.text::length with s being raw
    variable out as text is ""
    variable pending is empty-bytes with nothing
    variable index as number is 0
    repeat while index is less than width
        value here is nil.std.text::substring with s being raw, start being index, length being 1
        value escaped is here equals "%" and index plus 2 is less than width
            and (is-hex-pair with pair being (nil.std.text::substring with s being raw, start being index plus 1, length being 2))
        when escaped then
            value pair is nil.std.text::substring with s being raw, start being index plus 1, length being 2
            append (nil.std.core::Convert.from_hex with hex_str being pair) to pending
            set index to index plus 3
        otherwise
            set out to out plus (bytes-as-text with bytes being pending)
            set pending to empty-bytes with nothing
            when here equals "+" then
                set out to out plus " "
            otherwise
                set out to out plus here
            end when
            set index to index plus 1
        end when
    end repeat
    give out plus (bytes-as-text with bytes being pending)
end paragraph


empty-bytes returns array of number begin paragraph
    variable none-yet as array of number is []
    give none-yet
end paragraph


!!! --- Assemble decoded bytes into text, reading the UTF-8 as it goes.
!!!
!!! An ill-formed sequence becomes U+FFFD and the scan moves on by one byte,
!!! which is what the WHATWG encoding standard requires and what every browser
!!! does. The alternative — passing the byte through — produces text that is
!!! not valid UTF-8, and every later `length` or `substring` over it is then
!!! working on something the rest of the stack believes is impossible.

bytes-as-text receives bytes as array of number returns text begin paragraph
    value count is the length of bytes
    variable out as text is ""
    variable index as number is 0
    repeat while index is less than count
        value lead is bytes at index
        when lead is less than 128 then
            set out to out plus (nil.std.core::Convert.i32_to_char with code being lead)
            set index to index plus 1
        otherwise when lead is at least 194 and lead is at most 223 then
            value taken is take-sequence with bytes being bytes, index being index, follows being 1, mask being 31
            set out to out plus taken.0
            set index to index plus taken.1
        otherwise when lead is at least 224 and lead is at most 239 then
            value taken is take-sequence with bytes being bytes, index being index, follows being 2, mask being 15
            set out to out plus taken.0
            set index to index plus taken.1
        otherwise when lead is at least 240 and lead is at most 244 then
            value taken is take-sequence with bytes being bytes, index being index, follows being 3, mask being 7
            set out to out plus taken.0
            set index to index plus taken.1
        otherwise
            !!! 192, 193 and 245..255 can never lead a well-formed sequence.
            set out to out plus (replacement-character with nothing)
            set index to index plus 1
        end when
    end repeat
    give out
end paragraph


!!! --- Read one multi-byte sequence: the character it spells, and how many
!!! bytes to step over. An ill-formed one spells U+FFFD and steps over one,
!!! so the byte after it gets its own chance to begin a sequence.

take-sequence receives bytes as array of number, index as number, follows as number, mask as number
    returns tuple of (text, number) begin paragraph
    value count is the length of bytes
    when index plus follows is at least count then
        give ((replacement-character with nothing), 1)
    end when

    variable point as number is (bytes at index) bitwise and mask
    variable step as number is 1
    repeat while step is at most follows
        value next is bytes at (index plus step)
        when next is less than 128 or next is greater than 191 then
            give ((replacement-character with nothing), 1)
        end when
        set point to (point shifted left by 6) bitwise or (next bitwise and 63)
        set step to step plus 1
    end repeat

    !!! A surrogate half and anything past the last plane are not characters,
    !!! and an overlong encoding spells a point below its own range.
    value overlong is (follows equals 1 and point is less than 128)
        or (follows equals 2 and point is less than 2048)
        or (follows equals 3 and point is less than 65536)
    value surrogate is point is at least 55296 and point is at most 57343
    when overlong or surrogate or point is greater than 1114111 then
        give ((replacement-character with nothing), 1)
    end when

    give ((nil.std.core::Convert.i32_to_char with code being point), follows plus 1)
end paragraph


replacement-character returns text begin paragraph
    give nil.std.core::Convert.i32_to_char with code being 65533
end paragraph


is-hex-pair receives pair as text returns boolean begin paragraph
    give (is-hex-digit with digit being (nil.std.text::substring with s being pair, start being 0, length being 1))
        and (is-hex-digit with digit being (nil.std.text::substring with s being pair, start being 1, length being 1))
end paragraph


is-hex-digit receives digit as text returns boolean begin paragraph
    value folded is nil.std.text::to_lower with s being digit
    give folded equals "0" or folded equals "1" or folded equals "2" or folded equals "3"
        or folded equals "4" or folded equals "5" or folded equals "6" or folded equals "7"
        or folded equals "8" or folded equals "9" or folded equals "a" or folded equals "b"
        or folded equals "c" or folded equals "d" or folded equals "e" or folded equals "f"
end paragraph


!!! --- Encode one component for a query string.
!!!
!!! The unreserved set of RFC 3986 passes through; every other ASCII byte is
!!! escaped. Space becomes %20 and not `+`, because %20 is correct in both a
!!! path and a query while `+` is correct in neither except a form body.
!!!
!!! KNOWN LIMIT — non-ASCII bytes are passed through unescaped.
!!!
!!! Escaping one would mean reading its numeric value, and nothing reachable
!!! from Matrix can do that. nil.std.text slices by byte but has no byte-to-
!!! number conversion, and nil.std.core's char_to_i32 takes a whole Unicode
!!! scalar and *aborts the process* on a partial one — not a fault that can be
!!! caught, a fatal runtime error. So a byte of a multi-byte character cannot
!!! be inspected, and a multi-byte character cannot be isolated to inspect
!!! whole, because isolating it needs the same byte test.
!!!
!!! Decoding has no such hole: it works in byte values throughout and is
!!! correct for all of Unicode. This is the one direction that is short, and
!!! it closes the day nil.std.text grows a `byte_at`. Until then, a caller
!!! encoding text that may be non-ASCII should know that what comes back is a
!!! valid URL only if the input was ASCII.

public encode-component receives raw as text returns text begin paragraph
    value width is nil.std.text::length with s being raw
    variable out as text is ""
    variable index as number is 0
    repeat while index is less than width
        value here is nil.std.text::substring with s being raw, start being index, length being 1
        when is-unreserved with glyph being here then
            set out to out plus here
        otherwise
            value code is ascii-code-of with glyph being here
            when code is less than 0 then
                !!! Not an ASCII byte: see the limit above.
                set out to out plus here
            otherwise
                set out to out plus "%" plus (two-hex with code being code)
            end when
        end when
        set index to index plus 1
    end repeat
    give out
end paragraph


!!! --- A byte's value, or minus one when it is not an ASCII byte.
!!!
!!! By comparison rather than conversion, because conversion is the thing that
!!! aborts. Only runs for a byte already known not to be unreserved, so the
!!! common path through encode-component never reaches it.

ascii-code-of receives glyph as text returns number begin paragraph
    variable code as number is 0
    repeat while code is less than 128
        when glyph equals (nil.std.core::Convert.i32_to_char with code being code) then
            give code
        end when
        set code to code plus 1
    end repeat
    give 0 minus 1
end paragraph


is-unreserved receives glyph as text returns boolean begin paragraph
    value folded is nil.std.text::to_lower with s being glyph
    value is-glyph is folded is at least "a" and folded is at most "z"
    value is-digit is glyph is at least "0" and glyph is at most "9"
    give is-glyph or is-digit or glyph equals "-" or glyph equals "."
        or glyph equals "_" or glyph equals "~"
end paragraph


two-hex receives code as number returns text begin paragraph
    value high is (code over 16) remainder 16
    value low is code remainder 16
    give (hex-digit with amount being high) plus (hex-digit with amount being low)
end paragraph


hex-digit receives amount as number returns text begin paragraph
    give switch amount
        case 0 then stop switching with "0"
        case 1 then stop switching with "1"
        case 2 then stop switching with "2"
        case 3 then stop switching with "3"
        case 4 then stop switching with "4"
        case 5 then stop switching with "5"
        case 6 then stop switching with "6"
        case 7 then stop switching with "7"
        case 8 then stop switching with "8"
        case 9 then stop switching with "9"
        case 10 then stop switching with "A"
        case 11 then stop switching with "B"
        case 12 then stop switching with "C"
        case 13 then stop switching with "D"
        case 14 then stop switching with "E"
        otherwise stop switching with "F"
    end switch
end paragraph


!!! --- Split a query string or form body into decoded pairs.
!!!
!!! A bare key with no `=` is a parameter with an empty value, which is what
!!! every server does and what `?debug` means in practice. An empty segment,
!!! as in `a=1&&b=2`, is skipped rather than becoming a nameless parameter.

public parse-parameters receives raw as text returns array of query-parameter begin paragraph
    variable found as array of query-parameter is []
    when raw equals "" then
        give found
    end when
    value segments is split-on with input being raw, separator being "&"
    repeat for each segment in segments
        when not (segment equals "") then
            value raw-name is before-first with input being segment, separator being "="
            value raw-content is after-first with input being segment, separator being "="
            append build query-parameter with
                name being (decode-component with raw being raw-name),
                content being (decode-component with raw being raw-content)
                to found
        end when
    end repeat
    give found
end paragraph


!!! --- The first value for a name, or "" when it is absent.
!!! Query parameter names are case-sensitive, unlike header field names.

public parameter-value receives parameters as array of query-parameter, name as text returns text begin paragraph
    repeat for each parameter in parameters
        when parameter.name equals name then
            give parameter.content
        end when
    end repeat
    give ""
end paragraph


public has-parameter receives parameters as array of query-parameter, name as text returns boolean begin paragraph
    repeat for each parameter in parameters
        when parameter.name equals name then
            give true
        end when
    end repeat
    give false
end paragraph


!!! --- Every value for a repeated name, as `?tag=a&tag=b` sends.

public parameter-values receives parameters as array of query-parameter, name as text returns array of text begin paragraph
    variable found as array of text is []
    repeat for each parameter in parameters
        when parameter.name equals name then
            append parameter.content to found
        end when
    end repeat
    give found
end paragraph


!!! --- The path part of a request target, with the query removed and the
!!! percent-escapes decoded.

public target-path receives target as text returns text begin paragraph
    give decode-component with raw being (before-first with input being target, separator being "?")
end paragraph


!!! --- The query part of a request target, still encoded.
!!! Left encoded because `&` and `=` inside a value must survive until the
!!! split, and decoding first would lose them.

public target-query receives target as text returns text begin paragraph
    give after-first with input being target, separator being "?"
end paragraph
